{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/sarif-2.1/schema/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "fullName": "Trivy Vulnerability Scanner",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy",
          "name": "Trivy",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-29923",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "github.com/redis/go-redis: go-redis allows potential out of order responses when `CLIENT SETINFO` times out during connection establishment"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "go-redis is the official Redis client library for the Go programming language. Prior to 9.5.5, 9.6.3, and 9.7.3, go-redis potentially responds out of order when `CLIENT SETINFO` times out during connection establishment. This can happen when the client is configured to transmit its identity, there are network connectivity issues, or the client was configured with aggressive timeouts. The problem occurs for multiple use cases. For sticky connections, you receive persistent out-of-order responses for the lifetime of the connection. All commands in the pipeline receive incorrect responses. When used with the default ConnPool once a connection is returned after use with ConnPool#Put the read buffer will be checked and the connection will be marked as bad due to the unread data. This means that at most one out-of-order response before the connection is discarded. This issue is fixed in 9.5.5, 9.6.3, and 9.7.3. You can prevent the vulnerability by setting the flag DisableIndentity to true when constructing the client instance."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-29923",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-29923\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: github.com/redis/go-redis/v9\nFixed Version: 9.7.3, 9.6.3, 9.5.5\nLink: [CVE-2025-29923](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-29923)\ngo-redis is the official Redis client library for the Go programming language. Prior to 9.5.5, 9.6.3, and 9.7.3, go-redis potentially responds out of order when `CLIENT SETINFO` times out during connection establishment. This can happen when the client is configured to transmit its identity, there are network connectivity issues, or the client was configured with aggressive timeouts. The problem occurs for multiple use cases. For sticky connections, you receive persistent out-of-order responses for the lifetime of the connection. All commands in the pipeline receive incorrect responses. When used with the default ConnPool once a connection is returned after use with ConnPool#Put the read buffer will be checked and the connection will be marked as bad due to the unread data. This means that at most one out-of-order response before the connection is discarded. This issue is fixed in 9.5.5, 9.6.3, and 9.7.3. You can prevent the vulnerability by setting the flag DisableIndentity to true when constructing the client instance.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-29923**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|github.com/redis/go-redis/v9|9.7.3, 9.6.3, 9.5.5|[CVE-2025-29923](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-29923)|\n\ngo-redis is the official Redis client library for the Go programming language. Prior to 9.5.5, 9.6.3, and 9.7.3, go-redis potentially responds out of order when `CLIENT SETINFO` times out during connection establishment. This can happen when the client is configured to transmit its identity, there are network connectivity issues, or the client was configured with aggressive timeouts. The problem occurs for multiple use cases. For sticky connections, you receive persistent out-of-order responses for the lifetime of the connection. All commands in the pipeline receive incorrect responses. When used with the default ConnPool once a connection is returned after use with ConnPool#Put the read buffer will be checked and the connection will be marked as bad due to the unread data. This means that at most one out-of-order response before the connection is discarded. This issue is fixed in 9.5.5, 9.6.3, and 9.7.3. You can prevent the vulnerability by setting the flag DisableIndentity to true when constructing the client instance."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 3.7,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-39822",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "On Unix systems, opening a file in an os.Root improperly follows symli ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On Unix systems, opening a file in an os.Root improperly follows symlinks to locations outside of the Root when the final path component of the a path is a symbolic link and the path ends in /. For example, 'root.Open(\"symlink/\")' will open \"symlink\" even when \"symlink\" is a symbolic link pointing outside of the root."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-39822",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-39822\nSeverity: UNKNOWN\nPackage: stdlib\nFixed Version: 1.25.12, 1.26.5, 1.27.0-rc.2\nLink: [CVE-2026-39822](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-39822)\nOn Unix systems, opening a file in an os.Root improperly follows symlinks to locations outside of the Root when the final path component of the a path is a symbolic link and the path ends in /. For example, 'root.Open(\"symlink/\")' will open \"symlink\" even when \"symlink\" is a symbolic link pointing outside of the root.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-39822**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|UNKNOWN|stdlib|1.25.12, 1.26.5, 1.27.0-rc.2|[CVE-2026-39822](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-39822)|\n\nOn Unix systems, opening a file in an os.Root improperly follows symlinks to locations outside of the Root when the final path component of the a path is a symbolic link and the path ends in /. For example, 'root.Open(\"symlink/\")' will open \"symlink\" even when \"symlink\" is a symbolic link pointing outside of the root."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "UNKNOWN"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "version": "0.71.2"
        }
      },
